Subject: Represent base classes by the bytes the target gave them

bindgen stores each base class in a field of the base's own type, which is
`sizeof(base)` bytes wide. The target is not obliged to give the base that
many. Under the Itanium ABI a class which is not a POD for the purpose of
layout lends its tail padding to whatever the derived class lays out next:
given `struct B { B(); int i; char c; };`, `sizeof(B)` is eight but `B`'s data
ends at five, and `struct D : B { char d; };` puts `d` at offset five. A field
of type `B` puts it at eight, which leaves every member of `D` after the base
at the wrong offset and `D` four bytes too wide. The same thing happens between
one base and the next, and to a class holding a virtual base which its
most-derived class puts somewhere else. bindgen notices, after the fact -
`pad_struct` logs "Calculated wrong layout" - and writes the struct out anyway.
This is https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-bindgen/issues/380, reported again as
rust-lang/rust-bindgen#1116, rust-lang/rust-bindgen#1516 and
rust-lang/rust-bindgen#3095, and it is why `virtual_inheritance.hpp` in the
test suite carries a `--no-layout-tests` and a FIXME pointing at
rust-lang/rust-bindgen#465: `class D : C, B` there puts `bazz` at 28 and
bindgen put it at 32.

Which bytes the bases really occupy is something the target has already been
asked: it is the offset of the first member laid out after them, or, for a
class with no members of its own, the class's size. Where fields of the bases'
own types would run past that, they cannot be fields of those types at all -
Rust has no way to say "a `B`, but only the five bytes of it this class uses".
One opaque field spanning the bytes the bases do occupy leaves every member
after them at the offset the target reported, and the class at the size and
alignment the target gave it. The bases' types are what is lost, and only for
the classes bindgen was already writing out wrong; a class whose bases fit is
untouched. `gen_partialeq_impl` names those fields, so it is told when they are
not there and compares the one field which is.

`saw_base` had to stop aligning a packed struct's bases. `align_to_latest_field`
already returns early for a packed struct and then the base was aligned anyway,
which put the running offset past both where the target puts the base and where
`#[repr(packed)]` puts the field written for it - enough, now that the offset is
compared against something, to call a correct packed layout wrong.

Reading the answer back from the target rather than modelling an ABI is what
makes this portable. Nothing about the Itanium ABI is written down here, and the
MSVC ABI, which does not lend a base's tail padding to the derived class at all,
reports the offsets which say so and keeps its typed bases; where the two ABIs
put a virtual base differently, each is read back the same way.

Three things this does not reach.

A base moved by less than the room in front of the class's first member is not
seen. `struct B { B(); int i; char a, b, c; }; struct C { C(); char x, y; };
struct D : B, C { int z; };` really puts `C` at 7 and this still puts it at 8;
`z` is at 12 either way and the class ends at 16 either way, so nothing `D`
declares itself is wrong and there is nothing left to compare against. Catching
it takes the offset of each base, which libclang has reported only since 20.0,
through `clang_getOffsetOfBase`; `clang_Cursor_getOffsetOfField` answers for
fields alone, and bindgen asks clang-sys for `clang_11_0`. Reaching for the
newer call means raising that floor or asking at run time whether the symbol is
there, and then replacing this comparison with per-base offsets rather than
tightening it. Without asking clang the only bound left is to refuse every class
whose first base has room to spare, and that refuses far more than is ever
wrong: whether a base lends that room is the ABI's choice, and the sizes on
their own do not say.

A member which overlaps the bases rather than following them - which only
`[[no_unique_address]]` on an empty one does - leaves no run of bytes to write
at all. That is rust-lang/rust-bindgen#3438, a layout with no Rust shape; the
bases are left as they were and the log says so, rather than dropping them and
putting the member on top of what they hold.

The opaque field is bytes, so it carries the base's internal padding, which C++
need not initialize and which a derived `PartialEq` or `Debug` then reads. That
is what `_bindgen_opaque_blob` has always done for an opaque type, and what the
TODO on `helpers::blob` asks about; nothing here makes it better or worse.

